<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Tefilas HaMinchah [Laws of the Afternoon Prayer]<br>Halachah 7, Part 3 (§§ 32–55)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">Based on <span class="lr">LM I:49</span> (<i>LaShemesh Ohel Bahem</i>), continued</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 32 — Comforting Mourners; Death and Resurrection; the <i>Tzadik</i> Who Gathers Good Points</div>

<div class="p">Comforting mourners [<span class="h">nichum availim</span>]: the essential consolation is the consolation of Tziyon and Yerushalayim (<span class="mx">"May the Omnipresent console you among the mourners of Tziyon and Yerushalayim"</span>). The principle: since the light (= the soul) already existed in the world and was then withdrawn and died — there is hope to repair it (= <span class="h">techiyas hamasim</span>). Death derives from the death of the <span class="h">Melachim</span> [kings] at Creation's beginning through excessive light. Yet the worlds could not have come into existence otherwise. The difficulty in revealing His light was only due to its excess — no world can endure <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>. Hashem desired His light to shine specifically in <span class="h">Olam HaAsiyah</span> (= free will). Therefore He contracted through many contractions — all through <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span> (drawing, concealing, drawing again, many times), until creating the world. But all depends on man's <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Adam sinned immediately; Hashem worked on his tikun — he returned in <span class="h">teshuvah</span> (our Sages). Since then, death was decreed. But now death is a great good: <span class="mx">"behold, it was very good — this is death."</span> Due to Adam's and the generations' corruption, one cannot receive the needed light while alive. One must die — then be purified and arise at <span class="h">techiyas hamasim</span> with a wondrous new light. Since the light already existed and departed, it can be renewed — vessels purified to receive greater light (just as at Creation: through light appearing and withdrawing, vessels formed; = the death of the <span class="h">Melachim</span> and their tikun — precisely through shattering, the new <span class="h">Ohr Ma"H</span> emerged). All depends on <span class="h">teshuvah</span>: every arousal to return — however small — is never lost (<span class="mx">"no good will is ever lost"</span> [Zohar]). Through <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, sins become merits — vessels are thickened, then purified into wondrous new vessels for greater light. <span class="mx">"Descent for the purpose of ascent; sins transformed to merits; distance for the purpose of closeness."</span></div>

<div class="p">Not everyone merits this on his own — only through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who know this secret deeply. They awaken people to <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, gather every tiny good point and arousal, building wondrous tikun from them. Whoever desires life must seek the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who can work on his tikun — the very searching is an immense good. Hashem and the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> work constantly on Israel's souls — but need some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>. <span class="mx">"Open for Me as a needle's eye, and I shall open for you..."</span> The essential: do not mock or oppose those who work on your tikun. <span class="v">"Return to Hashem your G-d... from all the places where He has driven you... even if your dispersed are at the ends of the heavens, from there Hashem will gather you... and Hashem will circumcise your heart."</span> = <span class="v">"You return man to <i>dakah</i> [being crushed]"</span> — even from the crushing of soul. The <span class="h">tzadikim</span> can transform all descents to ascents, all sins to merits.</div>

<div class="p">This is our consolation for the Bais HaMikdash's destruction: since the light already existed (though destroyed twice), a <span class="h">reshimu</span> remains — through this, the third Bais HaMikdash will be built in even greater tikun: <span class="v">"the glory of this latter house shall be greater."</span> Like an artisan who breaks and remakes his vessel many times until perfected — <span class="v">"all these G-d does, two and three times with a man."</span> = building worlds and destroying them (= Breaking of Vessels and tikun). One must know: as long as any arousal exists, there is always hope through the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. Nullify one's own wisdom entirely before them: <span class="mx">"whoever is small is great."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 33 — <i>Matzah</i>: the Letter <i>Hai</i>; Bread of Poverty</div>

<div class="p">The difference between <span class="h">chametz</span> and <span class="h">matzah</span> is in the letter <span class="h">Hai</span>: <span class="mx">"between <i>chametz</i> and <i>matzah</i> there is only a <i>mashehu</i> [hairsbreadth]"</span> — the tiny space of the <span class="h">Hai</span>'s hollow. All = tikun of the contractions. The <span class="h">Hai</span> was a <span class="h">Dales</span> (poor and destitute, without knowledge) and was restored to <span class="h">Hai</span>. <span class="h">Matzah</span> = <span class="h">lechem oni</span> [bread of poverty] — its holiness is in the <span class="h">Hai</span> (called "poor" because the <span class="h">Dales</span> was poor). The essential tikun is <span class="h">teshuvah</span>: when bad thoughts overpower (= foolishness of heart = the essential poverty: <span class="mx">"there is no poor man except in <i>da'as</i>"</span>) = <span class="h">Dales</span>. When one overpowers and sanctifies thought → <span class="h">Hai</span>. <span class="h">Matzah</span> = holy thoughts (opposite <span class="h">chimutz hamoach</span> [fermenting of the brain] with bad thoughts). <span class="v">"This is the bread of poverty"</span>; <span class="v">"broken <i>matzah</i> — as is the poor man's way."</span> The essential tikun: emerging from poverty/lack of <span class="h">da'as</span> to sanctified <span class="h">da'as</span> = <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>. Then His <span class="h">Malchus</span> is revealed in completeness — both houses ascend. = <span class="h">Hai</span> was a <span class="h">Dales</span> and was restored to <span class="h">Hai</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 34 — <i>Kiddush HaChodesh</i>: "Bring an Atonement for Me" — Only Man Below Can Repair</div>

<div class="p">The moon's blemish and <span class="h">Kiddush HaChodesh</span> (§26). When the moon was not appeased, Hashem said: <span class="mx">"bring an atonement for Me, for I diminished the moon."</span> Meaning: the tikun can only come through Israel in this world of free will — through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> specifically. Hashem Himself, as it were, cannot repair it alone (the blemish came from absence of <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>). The essential tikun on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> (= <span class="h">Rosh Chodesh</span>, when Hashem says <span class="mx">"bring atonement for Me"</span>): the first of the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span>. The <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and their followers draw the tikun of the contractions through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> — repairing the moon's blemish upon which all worlds' tikun depends.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 35 — <i>Shavuos</i> = <i>Minchah</i> = Yitzchak; the World Was Suspended Until Torah</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Shavuos</span> (= receiving the Torah) = <span class="h">Minchah</span>. The three <span class="h">Regalim</span> = Avraham, Yitzchak, Yaakov: Pesach = Avraham; <span class="h">Shavuos</span> = Yitzchak (= <span class="h">Minchah</span>: Yitzchak established <span class="h">Minchah</span>); Sukkos = Yaakov. Torah from the mouth of <span class="h">Gevurah</span>. The <span class="h">shofar</span> of <span class="h">Matan Torah</span> = the <span class="h">shofar</span> of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> (= <span class="v">"You were revealed"</span>) = Yitzchak's ram. <span class="h">Shavuos</span> is the renewal of the world — the world was collapsing until the sixth of Sivan (our Sages: <span class="v">"the earth and all its inhabitants were dissolving — I established its pillars, Selah"</span>; <span class="v">"the sixth day"</span> = the world hung in suspense until Torah). Creation = contracting <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, overpowering bad thoughts with good, serving in measure — revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span>. At <span class="h">Matan Torah</span>, <span class="h">Malchus</span> was revealed face to face. Beforehand: warnings about the contractions (<span class="v">"lest they break through"</span>) and sanctity of thought (<span class="v">"be prepared for three days"</span> = sanctifying thought, especially regarding desire). All = <span class="h">Minchah</span>: the tikun of the contractions through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> before Shavuos.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 36 — <i>Sefiras HaOmer</i> = Tikun of the Contractions; <i>Minchas HaOmer</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Sefiras HaOmer</span>: its entire <span class="h">kavanos</span> = tikun of the contractions. Days of judgment (therefore no haircuts). All the <span class="h">Sefirah</span> days (preparation for Torah) = preparing to tikun the contractions so we can receive the great light in measure through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>. We count to the <span class="h">Omer</span> — a <span class="h">Minchah</span> offering of barley, <span class="h">Minchas Bikkurim</span>. <span class="h">Aviv</span> [spring] = tikun of <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> and <span class="h">tata'ah</span>. The essential tikun through <span class="h">Minchah</span>: the <span class="h">Minchas HaOmer</span> sweetens the judgments by repairing the contractions through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> = <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 37 — The Great Trembling at Sinai: Contracting the Immense Light</div>

<div class="p">The tremendous noise, fear, and awe at <span class="h">Matan Torah</span> (<span class="v">"the earth quaked, the heavens dripped"</span>; <span class="v">"thunder and lightning, a heavy cloud"</span>; <span class="v">"the people trembled"</span>) — all because immense contractions were needed. Contractions = judgments → great trembling (they thought the world was being destroyed). It was exceedingly difficult to contract such immense light. Their souls departed — Hashem drew upon them the <span class="h">tal</span> [dew] of revival (= <span class="h">techiyas hamasim</span>: when all will be repaired, each receiving <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>'s light in measure — the essential delight of <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span>). Then: <span class="v">"there shall be one day, known to Hashem, not day nor night — and toward evening there shall be light"</span> — <span class="h">Minchah</span> (= toward evening). From there He drew the tikun of the contractions so they could endure.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 38 — <i>Matan Torah</i>: The Entire Purpose; Vessels Through Contractions; Pesach and Shavuos</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Matan Torah</span>: the purpose of Creation. Every person was created for this — to tikun vessels and garments in this world to receive <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>'s light in measure (= the reward of <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span>: perceiving Him through purified vessels). All free will lies in making these vessels through the contractions. His light is <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, always shining — but cannot be received due to excess. After contraction: judgments arise → bad thoughts, foolishness, external wisdoms. Man's entire war. Torah was given for this — all measurements and contractions, vessels to receive and draw <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>. Many levels of contraction: the <span class="h">Chalal HaPanui</span> before Creation (itself with many levels — the <span class="h">Malbush</span>, <span class="h">Tehiru</span>, etc.); then the <span class="h">Kav</span> [line] through contractions producing <span class="h">Atzilus</span>, then a <span class="h">Parsah</span> producing <span class="h">Beriah</span>, then <span class="h">Yetzirah</span>, then <span class="h">Asiyah</span> (= man with free will). All lights drawn through many contractions within the <span class="h">Chalal HaPanui</span>. All passes through every Jew — each must tikun and draw the contractions through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Pesach: the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> revealed in immense glory (<span class="v">"I passed through the land of Egypt"</span> — the Targum: "I was revealed"; <span class="v">"I and not an angel"</span>). The light of Pesach night surpasses even Shabbos and Shavuos (the Ari"zal). But the essential tikun of contractions begins on the second day of Pesach (= <span class="h">Minchas HaOmer</span> of barley). Through the seven weeks of <span class="h">Sefirah</span>: tikun of contractions for each quality — until Shavuos: receiving the great light in measure = receiving the Torah. = Revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span>: <span class="v">"you shall be for Me a kingdom of <i>Kohanim</i>."</span> <span class="h">Shavuos</span> is called <span class="h">Atzeres</span> (= <span class="h">Malchus</span>: <span class="v">"this one shall rule my people"</span>).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 39 — Counting the Days to the <i>Omer</i>: <i>Minchah</i> = Yitzchak = Sweetening Judgments</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of counting = tikun of contractions: counting the days to the <span class="h">Omer</span> (= the <span class="h">Minchas HaOmer</span> of barley offered on the second day of Pesach). <span class="h">Minchah</span> = Yitzchak = tikun of contractions = sweetening judgments = tikun of everything = tikun of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 40 — The Poor Man's Offering: Even from the Greatest Poverty of <i>Da'as</i></div>

<div class="p">During the <span class="h">Sefirah</span>: purifying from Egypt's contamination (= <span class="h">teshuvah</span>). Every person, every generation, every year — purifying from one's contamination (= essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = essential preparation for Torah, for <span class="mx">"the deed is the essential, not the study"</span>). The essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> = <span class="h">tefilah l'ani</span> = <span class="h">korban Minchah</span> (= the poor man's offering: a tenth of an <span class="h">aifah</span> in utter destitution). Hashem illuminates each person's heart daily to awaken <span class="h">teshuvah</span> (<span class="lr">LM I:54</span>, "<span class="h">Zikaron</span>": He contracts Himself from <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> to <span class="h">Ain Tachlis</span>, hinting to each person). But any illumination from above feels like <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> to the person — usually one is aroused beyond measure. When one contracts: bad thoughts overpower (from the contractions' judgments = evil inclination). Many are driven from <span class="h">teshuvah</span> because of this.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore: the essential tikun of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = the poor man's offering in utter destitution. Nullify one's pride entirely; know one's lowliness in truth — but not to one's detriment! Not to distance further through recognizing one's lowliness and corruption, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — rather, trust in Hashem's kindness and His immense mercy: there is hope even for him, to draw close from wherever he is, through the poor man's offering (= <span class="h">Minchah</span>). Just as one who cannot afford an ox, ram, or lamb — not even birds — Hashem in His mercy found a remedy: bring at least a tenth of an <span class="h">aifah</span> of flour. This reveals: His mercy is never exhausted. Even if one cannot do <span class="h">teshuvah</span> like the great <span class="h">ba'alai teshuvah</span> of old (who fasted and afflicted themselves greatly) — one must never, ever hold back from <span class="h">teshuvah</span>! Even a single good point of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is exceedingly precious. When one contracts the light in one's heart, does not walk in greatness beyond oneself, guards one's thought, detaches from bad to good — though the <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is in the utmost smallness, it is precious to Hashem (= the poor man's offering that comes in utter destitution). As our Sages extolled the preciousness of the <span class="h">Minchas Oni</span>: <span class="v">"a soul who brings a <i>Minchah</i>."</span></div>

<div class="p">The essential offering now is <span class="h">tefilah</span> (= <span class="h">teshuvah</span>): <span class="v">"take with you words and return to Hashem"</span>; <span class="mx">"I ask of you only words."</span> The most precious: the prayer of the poor and destitute (as the Zohar extols on <span class="v">"a prayer for the poor man when he is wrapped"</span>). Even the poor man praying for physical needs — how much more so when one feels one's poverty in deeds (poor in <span class="h">da'as</span>: <span class="mx">"no one sins unless a spirit of foolishness enters him"</span>). All the more when both forms of poverty combine — poor in <span class="h">da'as</span> from corruption and poor in livelihood, confused by it all. When he reflects on his ways, heeds the hints Hashem calls him with daily, sees no hope — afflicted from every side, wife and children crying for bread — yet overpowers all this, pouring out his conversation before Hashem from the depths of his poverty: <span class="v">"a prayer for the poor man... before Hashem he pours out his conversation"</span> — this is the poor man's offering in utter destitution, exceedingly precious to Hashem. <span class="v">"He did not despise nor abhor the cry of the poor, nor hide His face from him — when he cried, He heard."</span> <span class="v">"I dwell with the crushed"</span>; <span class="v">"You return man to <i>dakah</i> — to the crushing of soul."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 41 — The Tenth of an <i>Aifah</i> Not Revealed to Avraham: the Secret of the Last Generations</div>

<div class="p">The Midrash: all offerings were revealed to Avraham Avinu except the <span class="h">asiris ha'aifah</span> [tenth of an <span class="h">aifah</span>]. This secret — the poor man's offering in utter destitution — cannot be grasped, because the final redemption depends on it: the essential tikun of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, never despairing even in the utmost destitution. <span class="mx">"One who brings much and one who brings little — so long as he directs his heart to Heaven."</span> This secret could not be revealed even to Avraham — only each person must understand himself that His mercy is unending. As the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> revealed in these generations. The essential revelation only through <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of these final generations (when the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> has greatly expanded). Hashem sends <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who are the aspect of <span class="h">hisnaitzetzus Mashiach</span> [sparks of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>] — David who always called himself poor: <span class="v">"I am poor and destitute."</span> <span class="h">Mashiach</span> = <span class="v">"a poor man riding on a donkey"</span>; <span class="v">"the poor of the nation shall take refuge in him."</span></div>

<div class="p">Some say even Avraham was shown this — but only through a hint. It cannot be revealed explicitly (lest free will be nullified). The <span class="h">Minchas Omer</span> of barley brought at the beginning of Israel's approach (= the beginning of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>) — barley is animal food (= the ultimate lack of <span class="h">da'as</span>, the ultimate poverty). From there specifically: the <span class="h">Minchah</span> of barley = <span class="h">teshuvah</span> and prayer in the utmost smallness and lowliness. Through this: the essential beginning of Israel's drawing close to their Father in Heaven — preparing through each day of the <span class="h">Sefirah</span> until receiving the Torah at Shavuos.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 42 — "He Adds One Forgiveness of His Own": the Boundless Secret of <i>Asiris Ha'Aifah</i></div>

<div class="p">The Midrash (Vayikra Rabbah): Rav Zeira on <span class="v">"to our G-d, for He abundantly pardons"</span> — Hashem adds one forgiveness of His own: the <span class="h">asiris ha'aifah</span>. He revealed to Avraham the mercy through greater offerings (ox, ram, birds) — but the abundant pardon of the <span class="h">asiris ha'aifah</span>: not revealed even to Avraham (or only as a hint). This is Hashem's own extra forgiveness: <span class="v">"He abundantly pardons."</span> It cannot be revealed explicitly — each must understand from himself, having mercy on himself, never despairing. The secret of the <span class="h">Korban Minchah</span> = the <span class="h">Minchas Omer</span> of barley. The Zohar: the <span class="h">Omer Minchah</span> is the test [<span class="h">b'diku</span>] of the <span class="h">Aishes Chayil</span> (= <span class="h">K'nesses Yisrael</span>) — each person's test: understanding through the hints of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> that there is hope through every good point (= <span class="h">Minchas Omer</span> of barley). Through this: the essential drawing close = receiving the Torah.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 43 — David: "Poor and Destitute Am I; My Heart Is Hollow — Like a Shadow Turning"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"For I am poor and destitute; my heart is hollow within me. Like a shadow as it turns, I am gone."</span> Rashi: <span class="v">"like a shadow as it turns"</span> = toward evening (= <span class="h">Minchah</span> time). David = <span class="h">teshuvah</span> (<span class="mx">"David was not fit for that deed, but to teach <i>teshuvah</i>"</span>) = prayer (<span class="v">"I am prayer"</span>). The essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span>-through-prayer = <span class="h">Minchah</span> (toward evening) = <span class="h">tefilah l'ani</span> = the poor man's offering. One must take to heart daily: the day turns, the sun departs. All one's days are a passing shadow — especially when most days have passed. One sees the light of <span class="h">da'as</span> (= the sun's light) departing through sins — poor in <span class="h">da'as</span>, yet retaining some <span class="h">reshimu</span> and <span class="h">nekudah</span> through which arousal to Hashem still exists (hearing the crier's voice and the hints). One must hurry to save one's soul — lest even this be forgotten (as Rabbainu teaches [<span class="lr">LM I:206</span>] on <span class="v">"I have strayed like a lost sheep... for Your <i>mitzvos</i> I have not forgotten"</span>). Through this: arousal to return in whatever way possible (= the poor man's offering) = <span class="h">Minchah</span> toward evening = <span class="v">"like a shadow turning."</span> Connected to: <span class="v">"poor and destitute am I; my heart is hollow"</span> — sensing one's poverty, contracting the light (<span class="v">"my heart is hollow"</span>), hurrying like a mighty man to save oneself before the day turns and the sun darkens.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 44 — The <i>Minchah</i> Offering Reaches from One End of the World to the Other</div>

<div class="p">The Midrash (Tzav): all offerings do not come in halves — but this one comes as half (half in the morning, half in the evening). Moreover: whoever offers it is considered as offering from one end of the world to the other — <span class="v">"from the rising of the sun to its setting... a pure <i>Minchah</i>."</span> The poor man's offering (= essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span>) pierces and descends to the depths, reaching from one end to the other. Every person in the world — even the most distant — the tikun of the poor man's offering reaches there: <span class="mx">"one who brings much and one who brings little..."</span> Through any arousal, any good point — one can return to Hashem. Pour out one's conversation like water: <span class="v">"pour out your heart like water before Hashem."</span> Even if it seems without intellect or proper <span class="h">kavanah</span> — pour everything before Hashem. This is exceedingly precious.</div>

<div class="p">The Midrash: it comes in halves — even the poor, destitute prayer (= <span class="h">Minchas Oni</span>) does not come complete (mixed with disruptive thoughts, seemingly unworthy of ascending). Yet this is the greatness of <span class="h">tefilah l'ani</span>: Hashem has mercy and receives it — since one senses one's poverty and overpowers to pray however one can, from the bitterness of destitution. The Midrash concludes: whoever offers it — as if offering from one end of the world to the other. Our Sages revealed: precisely such poor, destitute prayer reaches without limit — since one strengthens from such poverty of <span class="h">da'as</span> to still cry out before Hashem. This is the secret that cannot be grasped — how far His mercy extends, as long as one does not despair of crying out and praying.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 45 — Counting Each Day to the <i>Omer</i>: Every Day's Tikun Through <i>Tefilah</i></div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">Sefirah</span>: counting each day to the <span class="h">Omer</span> — preparing and purifying for Torah (= <span class="h">teshuvah</span>). Each day's tikun through the <span class="h">Minchas HaOmer</span> (= the poor man's offering in destitution): through this, every Jew who wishes to return can do so — through every good point, every day. One must count aloud each day (= drawing the tikun of that day's contraction through the speech of prayer). The essential tikun of each day's contraction through the poor man's offering = <span class="h">Minchas HaOmer</span>. Through this, each person can draw close from wherever he is — contracting mind and <span class="h">da'as</span> so that His <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> illuminates in measure, overpowering bad thoughts, serving through good qualities — revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span>. This = the <span class="h">Sefirah</span>'s entire <span class="h">kavanos</span>: repairing <span class="h">Malchus</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 46 — Shabbos <i>Minchah</i>: <i>Ra'ava d'Ra'avin</i>; Moshe's Departure; the Hidden Light</div>

<div class="p">The awesome holiness of Shabbos <span class="h">Minchah</span>: then Moshe departed (Zohar: the great revelation at <span class="h">Minchah</span> of Shabbos). The essential tikun of all worlds is through Shabbos (= <span class="h">Oraisa d'Atika S'tima'ah</span>). From there: the essential power of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> (<span class="v">"return to Hashem your G-d"</span>). All flaws derive from flawed contractions — not contracting properly, not guarding from the judgments' hold within them (bad thoughts). Adam sinned on <span class="h">Erev Shabbos</span> — Shabbos protected him: <span class="mx">"Shabbos sheltered Adam."</span> Shabbos = <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span> (the essential tikun will be then). In Shabbos: rest — no creation of worlds (= qualities). Shabbos = the purpose of all Creation: revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span>. Had Adam not sinned on the first <span class="h">Erev Shabbos</span>, all would have been repaired in the first Shabbos. Even after his sin, tikun was through Shabbos. In Shabbos: all great lights shine through pure, holy contractions without any hold of judgment = <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span>. The <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who are Shabbos of all days (never sinning, yet constantly in <span class="h">teshuvah</span> on their prior perception) draw Shabbos's holiness = tikun of contractions = awakening of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Shabbos <span class="h">Minchah</span>: the worlds ascend to the ultimate height — <span class="h">tlisar tikunai dikna</span>, <span class="h">Ra'ava d'Ra'avin</span> [Will of Wills]. Near the departure of Shabbos's light, when weekday begins (= the contraction of Creation) — the prior tikun of Shabbos sweetens all contractions. All judgments in the contractions are nullified and sweetened at their root — enabling the weekday creation in tikun. This occurs every Shabbos: Israel keeps Shabbos (= the hidden treasure in His treasury). The <span class="h">ohr haganuz</span> [hidden light] from the six days of Creation shines through the hidden <span class="h">tzadikim</span> on Shabbos into each person's heart — according to one's effort and closeness to the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and to Shabbos. Therefore the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> customarily say Torah on Shabbos (especially the Third Meal) — awakening the world to <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, whose essential arousal is through Shabbos <span class="h">Minchah</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 47 — Moshe's Departure at Shabbos <i>Minchah</i>: Greater in Death Than in Life</div>

<div class="p">Moshe, who gave us the Torah, departed at Shabbos <span class="h">Minchah</span> in <span class="h">Ra'ava d'Ra'avin</span>. His entire exertion at the end was to instill Torah's observance in Israel (the hardest of all). In the final <span class="h">parshiyos</span> of Devarim: <span class="v">"gather to me... for I know that after my death you will surely become corrupt"</span>; <span class="v">"now write this song."</span> Hashem granted: his departure at Shabbos <span class="h">Minchah</span> in <span class="h">Ra'ava d'Ra'avin</span>. Death derives from the death of the <span class="h">Melachim</span> (excessive light, flawed contractions) — Adam should have repaired this but sinned → death was decreed. Now death is a great good: since the soul already existed and departed, tikun is possible (§32). The great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> (= Moshe) departed at Shabbos <span class="h">Minchah</span>: the ultimate tikun of the supernal contractions, receiving awesome light in tikun. Through his soul's ascent there, he draws the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span>'s revelation into every Jew's heart in tikun — until ultimately bringing all Israel to <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. <span class="mx">"The <i>tzadikim</i> are greater in death than in life."</span></div>

<div class="p">In life, they labored to reveal His <span class="h">Malchus</span> — drawing <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>'s light in measured contractions, serving in gradation: <span class="v">"Your <i>chasidim</i> shall bless You; the <i>kavod</i> of Your <i>Malchus</i> they shall speak."</span> The Adversary fights fiercely. What they could not complete in life, they complete after death — immediately entering eternal life of <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span> (= <span class="h">techiyas hamasim</span>). Even there they serve Hashem (our Sages). Their work: drawing the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> into all Israel's hearts in tikun of the contractions — revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span> to all (the purpose of Creation). This is the greatness of visiting <span class="h">tzadikim</span>'s graves — especially on <span class="h">Erev Rosh HaShanah</span>: drawing the tikun of Creation renewed, repairing Adam's sin through the tikun of the contractions — accomplished through the great departed <span class="h">tzadikim</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 48 — <i>Luchos</i> and Broken <i>Luchos</i>: Shattering for the Sake of Tikun</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">Luchos</span> and broken <span class="h">Luchos</span> — at the Torah's conclusion, speaking of Moshe's greatness and departure: <span class="v">"Moshe died there"</span>; <span class="v">"the mighty hand and great awe that Moshe performed before all Israel"</span> — our Sages: he broke the <span class="h">Luchos</span> before their eyes. Broken <span class="h">Luchos</span> = Breaking of Vessels = death of the <span class="h">Melachim</span>. But through the shattering comes the essential tikun: since they already existed and were broken — tikun is possible. <span class="mx">"The <i>Luchos</i> and broken <i>Luchos</i> rest in the Ark."</span> The second <span class="h">Luchos</span> (received in greater tikun, called "good") could only exist because the first had already existed and been broken. It is hard for perfection to emerge on the first attempt — = building worlds and destroying them. = Death and resurrection. But the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> do not truly die: <span class="mx">"Moshe did not die"</span> (Zohar); Yaakov Avinu did not die. Their departure = ascent into <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> to draw light back in tikun — just as at Creation's beginning (light revealed, concealed, revealed). But now, with <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> through the perfect <span class="h">tzadikim</span>'s departure into eternal life. <span class="v">"You shall lie down with your fathers and arise"</span> — immediately upon departing, the <span class="h">tzadik</span> begins eternal life (= <span class="h">techiyas hamasim</span>). All to reveal <span class="h">Malchus</span> — drawing the illumination of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> until all Israel returns in truth. <span class="h">Amain, kain y'hi ratzon</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 49 — Three Times "<i>Tzidkas'cha</i>" at Shabbos <i>Minchah</i>: Yosef, Moshe, David</div>

<div class="p">Three times <span class="v">"<i>Tzidkas'cha</i>"</span> at Shabbos <span class="h">Minchah</span>: three <span class="h">tzadikim</span> departed then — Yosef, Moshe, David. The essential tikun through <span class="h">tzedakah</span> (= tikun of the <span class="h">Hai</span>: <span class="h">Dales</span> restored to <span class="h">Hai</span> through drawing holiness — overpowering holy thoughts against bad thoughts — through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who departed). Three times <span class="h">Tzidkas'cha</span> = tikun through <span class="h">tzedakah</span> = tikun of the <span class="h">Hai</span> = <span class="h">teshuvah</span>: from the greatest poverty and foolishness, returning through the departed <span class="h">tzadikim</span>'s power → making <span class="h">Dales</span> into <span class="h">Hai</span>, <span class="h">Tzedek</span> into <span class="h">Tzedakah</span>. Drawing the ten types of melody = joy in Hashem in the joy of Shabbos — especially the Third Meal at Shabbos <span class="h">Minchah</span>: <span class="mx">"<i>Chadu</i> now, at this hour of <i>Ra'ava</i>..."</span> <span class="mx">"Now, at <i>Minchah</i>, in joy..."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 50 — Pesach Night: the Children's Questions = Contraction; the Wise Son vs. the Wicked Son</div>

<div class="p">On Pesach night, the children must ask <span class="h">kushyos</span> [questions] (= <span class="h">Mah Nishtanah</span>). The <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> shines in great light → yet contraction is needed. The <span class="h">kushyos</span> = contraction (= concealment of knowledge). Through asking, one draws the contraction of light — preventing excessive enthusiasm. But the question must be in the way of wisdom (= the wise son: <span class="v">"what are the testimonies and statutes?"</span> — acknowledging the Torah, inquiring what it alludes to). Not like the wicked son! The contraction must be for the sake of revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span> — not for hiding from His light, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. Four questions (= the <span class="h">Hai</span> was a <span class="h">Dales</span> — four = poverty and hiddenness of <span class="h">da'as</span>). Our work: making <span class="h">Dales</span> into <span class="h">Hai</span>. = <span class="v">"This is the bread of poverty"</span> — the broken <span class="h">matzah</span> upon which we answer many things = <span class="h">D"I</span> [<span class="h">Dales</span>-<span class="h">Yud</span>] that our fathers ate.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 51 — Song Simple, Double, Triple, Quadruple = the Ten Types of Melody</div>

<div class="p">When thought is sanctified until <span class="h">Malchus</span> is revealed and the <span class="h">Hai</span> is repaired (through the <span class="h">Yud</span>'s ten holy aspects) — then: <span class="h">shir pashut, kaful, m'shulash, m'ruba</span> [song simple, double, triple, quadruple] = the ten types of melody = <span class="v">"the song shall be yours on the night of the sanctified festival"</span> = Pesach night (<span class="h">Hallel</span> completed many times). Song simple (= <span class="h">Yud</span>): the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span>, utterly simple. Song double (= <span class="h">Y-H</span>): contracting it (<span class="h">Hai</span> = <span class="h">Binah</span> = beginning of contraction). Song triple (= <span class="h">Y-H-V</span>): drawing wisdom into the heart, creating qualities/worlds (<span class="h">Vav</span> = the six days of building); there the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil takes hold — one must overpower toward good. Song quadruple (= <span class="h">Y-H-V-H</span>): revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span> (the <span class="h">Dales</span>). = The ten types of melody: the <span class="h">Yud</span> drawn into the <span class="h">Dales</span> making <span class="h">Hai</span>.</div>

<div class="p">The essential: at each stage, returning to remember the <span class="h">Yud</span> (= <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span>). Even while contracting (<span class="h">Hai</span>) — bind the <span class="h">Yud</span> there. Through this: overpowering bad with good by always remembering Hashem: <span class="v">"when my soul was wrapped about me, I remembered Hashem."</span> The contraction was only to serve Him in measure and reveal <span class="h">Malchus</span>. = <span class="v">"Turn your eyes from before Me."</span> The contraction = so one accepts <span class="h">Malchus</span>, then yearns even more (the obstacle awakens desire). = <span class="v">"Who is this ascending?"</span> After accepting <span class="h">Malchus</span> fully (= <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>), one can receive the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span>'s enthusiasm in tikun (= <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> ascending) — what was previously <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> (lacking vessels) now has vessels through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 52 — <i>Teshuvah</i> Reaches the Throne of <i>Kavod</i>; the <i>Tzadik</i> as the True <i>Ba'al Teshuvah</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Teshuvah</span> reaches the Throne of <span class="h">Kavod</span> (= <span class="h">Oraisa d'Atika S'tima'ah</span>). Precisely through descending to low, coarse places — then rising through the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s power — wondrous vessels are formed that even complete <span class="h">tzadikim</span> cannot receive (lacking such vessels). The lower one descends, the more wondrous the vessels formed upon rising. All through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — who are themselves <span class="h">ba'alai teshuvah</span>: though never tasting sin, they constantly return on their prior perception (<span class="lr">LM I:6</span>, "<span class="h">Krah es Yehoshua</span>"). Often they intentionally descend to very low places to raise souls and bring them to <span class="h">teshuvah</span> — Rabbainu said: <span class="mx">"my hands are soiled with blood... to purify."</span> Through these <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, whoever grasps them can ascend from the depths. They are the true <span class="h">ba'alai teshuvah</span> — the masters [<span class="h">b'alim</span>] of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. Whoever desires to return must receive from them.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 53 — Joy: Song Simple/Double/Triple/Quadruple Through Simchah; "Dark Am I, yet Lovely"</div>

<div class="p">The song (= ten types of melody) is made through <span class="h">simchah</span> — one must strengthen to rejoice: <span class="mx">"serve Hashem with joy."</span> Joy = song and melody = the ten types of melody. One must gladden oneself through all counsel: the essential — that He did not make me a gentile; that one drew close to true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and was separated from the opponents; that one finds good points in oneself (<span class="lr">LM I:282</span>, "<span class="h">Azamrah</span>").</div>

<div class="p">Song simple (= <span class="h">Yud</span>): rejoicing that one is a Jew, believing in Hashem (utterly simple). Every Jew is called <span class="h">Ish Y'hudi</span> [a Jewish man] — named for the <span class="h">Yud</span>: <span class="v">"Your people are all <i>tzadikim</i>"</span> (named for the <span class="h">Yud</span>). Song double (= <span class="h">Y-H</span>): adding joy for drawing close to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. The <span class="h">Hai</span> = <span class="h">Binah</span> = contemplating His greatness (the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s work: instilling perception of G-dliness). Song triple (= <span class="h">Y-H-V</span>): finding good points in oneself (how can one not have done some good?). Three joys: not being a gentile; being separated from opponents of the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>; having good points. The Midrash (Shir HaShirim): <span class="v">"dark am I yet lovely"</span> — "dark in my deeds, lovely in my forefathers' deeds" (= song simple: joy in being Avraham/Yitzchak/Yaakov's children); "dark in my deeds, lovely in the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>'s deeds" (= song double: not opposing the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>); "dark in the Calf's deed, lovely in the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>'s deed" (= song triple: good points found even in my deeds). Through all this joy — one strengthens to snatch good even now: Torah, prayer, <span class="h">tzedakah</span>, good deeds (= accepting <span class="h">Malchus</span>) = song quadruple (= <span class="h">Y-H-V-H</span> complete). Joy completed in ten types of melody. Fortunate is one who grasps this.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 54 — R' Akiva's Students: Why They Died During <i>Sefirah</i>; Love and Unity for Torah</div>

<div class="p">R' Akiva's students died between Pesach and Shavuos (= <span class="h">Sefirah</span>). They were great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who needed to transmit the Torah from R' Akiva (who was like Moshe — our Sages: "a prophet like him did not arise, but a sage did — R' Akiva"; Moshe could not fathom R' Akiva's Torah). Their flaw: no love and fellowship among them. They needed to transmit Torah and tikun contractions/vessels so all Israel could receive <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>'s light — through every letter, word, <span class="h">parashah</span>, and <span class="h">halachah</span> of Torah, vessels and contractions are formed to reveal <span class="h">Malchus</span> (<span class="lr">LM I:30</span>, "<span class="h">Mishrah d'Sakina</span>"). Torah scholars must labor to produce true <span class="h">chiddushim</span> — every new word of Torah creates a vessel to draw His light further: <span class="v">"new heavens and a new earth"</span> created through Torah <span class="h">chiddushim</span> (Zohar). = <span class="v">"to plant heavens and found the earth... to say to Tziyon: 'you are My people.'"</span></div>

<div class="p">To tikun the contractions and draw <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>'s light in measure requires many souls united in love — many vessels. Torah could not be given until 600,000 gathered in love (Rashi: <span class="v">"Israel encamped there"</span> — as one man). <span class="mx">"The Shechinah does not rest on fewer than 22,000."</span> <span class="h">Hashraas haShechinah</span> needs a gathering of many souls (= the census before <span class="h">Matan Torah</span> and after the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>). Therefore <span class="h">Parshas Bamidbar</span> (= the census) is read before Shavuos: <span class="mx">"count them and stop"</span> [= <span class="h">manu v'itz'ru</span>]. R' Akiva's students died during <span class="h">Sefirah</span> (when the contractions need tikun for Torah's reception), because they needed to transmit R' Akiva's (= Moshe's) Torah — but lacked love.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 55 — The Five Replacement Students; the Letter <i>Hai</i></div>

<div class="p">The world was desolate — "like the day the <span class="h">Luchos</span> were broken" — until R' Akiva taught the Sages of the south: R' Shimon ben Yochai and others — five students who sustained the Torah. Why five? All tikun of drawing Torah (= tikun of the contractions) is essentially tikun of the <span class="h">Hai</span> (as explained in the teaching). = The five books of Torah [<span class="h">Chamishah Chumshay Torah</span>]. Therefore five students — engaged in tikun of the <span class="h">Hai</span>.</div>

